choices={
'one_sample':[
 ('Which tail?',r'<p>For \(H_1:\mu&lt;\mu_0\), which t values provide evidence against H₀?</p>',{'A':'Large positive values.','B':'Sufficiently negative values.','C':'Only values close to zero.','D':'All non-zero values.'},'B','<p>The statistic is observed mean minus null mean, divided by a positive standard error.</p>'),
 ('A non-significant result','<p>A valid t-test does not reject H₀ at 5%. Which conclusion is justified?</p>',{'A':'H₀ has been proved.','B':'There is a 95% probability that H₀ is true.','C':'The data provide insufficient evidence against H₀ at 5%.','D':'The populations must have zero variance.'},'C','<p>Failure to reject is a statement about evidence under the procedure, not proof of the null.</p>')],
'normal':[
 ('Why no normal population assumption?','<p>For independent large samples with unknown finite variances, what justifies the normal approximation to a difference of sample means?</p>',{'A':'The central limit theorem, with a suitable random independent sampling design.','B':'Every large data set is exactly normal.','C':'The two population variances must be equal.','D':'All individual readings are independent of the population.'},'A','<p>The approximation concerns the sample means, not necessarily the original observations.</p>'),
 ('Small samples, known variances','<p>Two independent normal populations have known variances. Which method is appropriate for a mean-difference interval, even with small samples?</p>',{'A':'Always use a pooled t interval.','B':'Use an exact normal procedure with known-variance standard error.','C':'Treat the readings as paired.','D':'There is no valid interval.'},'B','<p>No variance is estimated, so the normal standardisation is exact under the stated model.</p>')],
'pooled':[
 ('What makes pooling appropriate?','<p>Which assumptions support the standard small-sample pooled t-test?</p>',{'A':'Two dependent samples with unequal variances.','B':'Independent random samples from normal populations with a common unknown variance.','C':'Equal observed sample means only.','D':'Large sample sizes alone.'},'B','<p>Pooling estimates a common variance; normality and independence supply the t model.</p>'),
 ('Which degrees of freedom?','<p>Independent samples have sizes 7 and 11. How many degrees of freedom does the pooled t statistic have?</p>',{'A':'6.','B':'10.','C':'16.','D':'18.'},'C','<p>The sum is (7−1)+(11−1)=16.</p>')],
'paired':[
 ('The correct normality assumption','<p>For a small-sample paired t-test, which normality assumption is required?</p>',{'A':'The population of within-pair differences is normal.','B':'The two marginal populations must be identical.','C':'Only the before readings are normal.','D':'All 2n readings are mutually independent.'},'A','<p>The t-test is performed on one sample of differences, with independent pairs.</p>'),
 ('A common correction','<p>Both readings in one pair were 4 units too high. After correcting them, what happens to the paired t statistic?</p>',{'A':'It increases by 4.','B':'Its degrees of freedom double.','C':'It is unchanged because the within-pair difference is unchanged.','D':'It must become zero.'},'C','<p>Subtracting the same amount from both members cancels in their difference.</p>')],
'intervals':[
 ('Confidence refers to the procedure','<p>What does 95% confidence mean under a valid model?</p>',{'A':'95% of individual observations lie in the mean interval.','B':'The fixed mean changes with probability 0.95.','C':'95% of intervals made by the procedure in repeated sampling would contain the fixed mean.','D':'The sample mean is correct with probability 0.95.'},'C','<p>Coverage describes repeated intervals; the parameter itself is fixed.</p>'),
 ('Match the tails','<p>Which test directly corresponds to a central 90% confidence interval under the same model?</p>',{'A':'A one-sided 10% test.','B':'A two-sided 10% test, with the stated boundary convention.','C':'A two-sided 5% test.','D':'Every test at every significance level.'},'B','<p>The excluded total tail probability is 10%, split into 5% at each end.</p>')],
'differences':[
 ('Zero inside','<p>A central 95% interval for a mean difference has zero strictly inside. What follows for the matching two-sided 5% test?</p>',{'A':'Equality is proved.','B':'Every A observation equals every B observation.','C':'Do not reject equality of population means.','D':'Always reject equality.'},'C','<p>Zero is compatible with the interval; compatibility is not proof.</p>'),
 ('Pairing is a different design','<p>The same nine devices are measured before and after a change. Which interval estimates the population mean change?</p>',{'A':'A pooled t interval treating 18 readings as independent.','B':'A one-mean t interval for nine paired differences, with 8 degrees of freedom.','C':'A normal interval with all variances set to zero.','D':'An interval for the sample median only.'},'B','<p>Retain the matching and analyse one difference per independent device.</p>')]
}
